exits the market (even temporarily, say via stockout), the aftermath can be telling. Sometimes another
competitor steps up to become the new leader, but other times a period of price chaos ensues as
remaining players jostle for position. A common pattern is a margin restoration window after an
aggressive leader exits – competitors may quietly raise prices to recover margins once the price-cutting
aggressor is gone. For instance, when a ruthless discounter selling at a loss runs out of stock, more
rational sellers often seize the chance to lift prices back to profitable levels. On the flip side, if a stable
high-price leader exits, followers might splinter – one or two may take the opportunity to grab market
share by underpricing, creating a new round of competition until a new equilibrium is found.
Aggressive vs. Cooperative Competitors: An aggressive competitor is one that prioritizes market
share or strategic goals over short-term profit, often by undercutting prices relentlessly. Aggressors
initiate price cuts, sometimes irrationally or at unsustainable levels, effectively “weaponizing” price
. They differ from rational competitors in that their behavior doesn’t align with maximizing
immediate profit – they may be willing to lose money or accept tiny margins to achieve their aims (e.g.
driving out rivals, or rapidly acquiring customers). In contrast, cooperative competitors (whether by
tacit agreement or mutual understanding) avoid rocking the boat on price. They prefer stable or slowly
varying prices that allow all players to earn a decent margin. These competitors behave almost as if
colluding (though without explicit agreement), each refraining from aggressive cuts knowing it would
likely trigger retaliation and hurt everyone. For example, in UK petrol retail it has been observed that
stations in a town often move prices in unison or hold them steady, a form of tacit coordination where
no one station wants to trigger a price war for a few extra customers . Cooperative behavior can
also be seen when competitors subtly signal each other – e.g. a small price increase by one, waiting to
see if others match (common in the airline industry’s attempts to raise fares).
Crucially, aggressors differ from rational players in motive and response to stimuli. A rational
competitor might cut price only when sales drop or costs fall, and will raise price when costs rise or a
competitor exits – moves grounded in profit logic. An aggressor might cut price simply to test others’
resolve or to gain a reputation as a “low price” player. They may continue even when it’s clearly
unprofitable. This “irrational” behavior often confounds rational rivals. For instance, in one analysis fully
half of companies that started price wars claimed it was by mistake, misreading a competitor’s
move or data signal . Aggressors can provoke such mistakes. A rational firm might misinterpret
an aggressor’s unsustainably low price as a long-term shift and overreact by slashing its own prices,
thereby amplifying the aggressor’s impact. For this reason, it’s vital to determine if a competitor’s price
drop is a short-term “gambit” or a permanent shift – price wars often begin when simple promotions
are misunderstood as strategic changes .
Temporary Disruptors vs. Long-Term Incumbents: Marketplace dynamics also feature temporary or
opportunistic players versus established incumbents. Temporary disruptors could be new entrants,
one-time sellers, or existing competitors with a short-term agenda (e.g. clearing excess inventory). They
might enter a listing or market with a shockingly low price – sometimes below cost – for a short
period. A classic example is a seller with overstock who floods the market at clearance prices to unload
inventory (an inventory-driven desperation pricing scenario). This seller might disappear after the
stock is gone or prices return to normal once the goal is achieved. Long-term incumbents, on the
other hand, focus on sustainable pricing. They often have higher costs or brand value to protect and
won’t match fire-sale prices if it jeopardizes their business. Instead, incumbents may temporarily
tolerate a loss of sales until the disruptor exits, then raise prices to restore margins. An intelligent
pricing strategy recognizes these cases: fighting a temporary disruptor head-on with price cuts can be
pointless if the disruptor’s horizon is short. It may be wiser to wait them out or use non-price tactics
(better service, bundles, etc.) to differentiate during the disruptor’s brief play. Indeed, research suggests
that if a competitor’s low-price move threatens only a small portion of your sales, the cost of matching
