In summary, markets exhibit a spectrum of roles – leaders set pace (up or down), followers react (with
varying lag), aggressors push extremes, cooperators seek stability, disruptors come and go, incumbents
endure. These roles influence group pricing: a strong leader can stabilize prices (competitors all
follow its increases or decreases in tandem ), whereas a rogue aggressor can destabilize an
otherwise cooperative market by forcing a downward spiral. And when a leader exits, followers either
find a new reference or engage in short-term jostling until a new order emerges. Recognizing who is
who in your competitive landscape is the first step to anticipating pricing moves and responding wisely.
Known Competitive Scenarios and Their Characteristics
In dynamic marketplaces, certain competitive scenarios occur again and again. Designing a pricing
system that can identify these patterns is crucial. Below is a catalog of recurring scenarios, each with
typical precursors (signals), evolutionary patterns, and notes on intervention strategies. We focus on
scenarios common in e-commerce (like Amazon UK) but note parallels in other industries for context.
1. Single Aggressor vs. Passive Followers
Description: One competitor (the aggressor) consistently undercuts prices, while the rest mostly hold
their prices or only adjust reluctantly. The aggressor might be a new entrant, a competitor with excess
stock, or simply a player pursuing market share at all costs. Other sellers – the passive followers – prefer
to maintain price stability and margin, adjusting only when absolutely necessary.
Signals: Look for one seller who nearly always has the lowest price and is often the first to drop price
further. Their price changes are frequent and mostly downward. They may even price below a logical
floor (e.g. under known cost). Other competitors’ prices appear static or change only after delays. For
example, you might see that Seller A changes price daily and is always £0.10 below the next lowest,
whereas Sellers B, C rarely change price and usually sit at higher levels. The aggressor’s timing
(initiating moves), frequency (many changes), and directionality (mostly cuts) stand out.
Evolution: If unchecked, the aggressor gradually drives the market price down. Passive followers might
initially ignore minor cuts (hoping it’s temporary), but as the price gap grows, they face a dilemma:
either start matching (entering a price war) or cede sales to the aggressor. Often the aggressor gains
market share until a tipping point: either they run out of steam (stock out or cannot sustain losses) or a
major follower finally reacts aggressively. Many times, passive players hope the aggressor will burn
out. Indeed, aggressive undercutting is often short-lived – it’s a “common (and often short-lived) tactic”
in online retail . If the aggressor does burn out (e.g. sells through inventory or gets suspended), a
margin restoration phase follows where remaining sellers raise prices back toward normal. If not, and
followers start matching, the scenario can escalate into a broader price war (see Scenario 3).
Intervention: When to react and how? If you identify a lone aggressor with unsustainable pricing, it
may be profitable not to match immediately. Monitoring is key: is their price cut a short promotion or a
permanent change ? If likely short-term (e.g. a seller clearing stock or with poor feedback that won’t
win the Amazon Buy Box regardless of price due to slow delivery), the best response might be patience
or targeted price adjustments only for the most price-sensitive customers. Maintaining your price might
mean losing some sales now, but preserves margin and avoids validating the aggressor’s strategy. On
Amazon, also consider delivery time advantages: if the aggressor offers 5-day shipping and you offer
