2. Multiple Independent Leaders (Fragmented Competition)
Description: Instead of one clear price leader, the market has a few strong competitors each leading on
different products or segments. No single firm dominates across the board; leadership is fragmented.
Each leader might ignore the others in certain categories and set prices based on its own strategy (cost
plus, target margin, etc.), leading to divergent pricing. Essentially, you have multiple “mini-markets”
where different players take the lead.
Signals: This scenario is characterized by price dispersion and lack of a consistent pattern of one
following another. You might observe that for Product X, Seller B always has the best price (others follow
B if they change), but for Product Y, Seller C is usually lowest and others follow C. Each competitor might
“own” a niche – possibly due to different cost structures or inventory positions. For instance, a retailer
might lead on electronics while another leads on home goods. Timing signals: price changes occur, but
not in a single sequence; instead, different items see different initiators. Followers exist, but they follow
different leaders depending on the item.
Evolution: Fragmented competition often keeps prices relatively stable or rational within each
fragment because each mini-leader often sets price to maintain margin (they don’t want to destroy their
own profitability across their category). However, if one leader starts encroaching on another’s domain
(say one firm tries to lead in a category typically led by another by slashing price there), it can trigger
skirmishes localized to that segment. Over time, fragments can consolidate: one player might gain
strength across segments and become a more general leader, or conversely a new entrant might
disrupt multiple segments and fragment things further. It’s also possible for tacit coordination to
develop in each segment, as the main players in that slice of the market signal each other to avoid
ruinous competition (see Scenario 8 on tacit collusion).
Intervention: In a fragmented scenario, identify where you are leader vs. follower. For the products
where you have cost or inventory advantages (your “home turf”), you can set the tone – perhaps be a bit
more aggressive in pricing to build share, knowing others will react to you. Where you’re not the leader,
resist the urge to knee-jerk match every move – instead, watch the leader’s behavior and strategy. If
they raise price, that’s an opportunity for you to either hold (and gain share) or follow if margins are
tight. If they cut price, evaluate if it’s temporary or linked to something (promo, new stock arrival).
Because multiple leaders exist, avoid blanket rules; treat each segment independently. Automation
should be segment-aware – it might follow competitor A’s price changes on one SKU but competitor B’s
on another. Human oversight is useful to label who the “benchmark competitor” is for each category
and ensure the system doesn’t mix signals. The key risk here is overreaction: if you drop prices across
the board in response to one competitor’s move in one segment, you can trigger unnecessary margin
loss in others. Instead, intervene in a targeted way. This scenario often doesn’t require aggressive
intervention unless a competitor tries to “steal” leadership in your core segment, in which case be
prepared to defend that specific turf (with temporary price drops, promotional bundles, etc.) – but still
