3. Price War Triggered by an Irrational Actor
Description: A classic price war often starts when one competitor – sometimes new or desperate –
makes an aggressive price cut that others view as irrational or threatening. This could be a one-off move
that snowballs: Company X, perhaps misreading market conditions, slashes price; Company Y retaliates
by undercutting X; soon all players are repeatedly lowering prices in rapid succession. “Price wars
happen when companies intentionally undercut each other… spiraling into a price war” . Often the
instigator might not even intend a full war – they may have miscalculated, but once others respond in
kind, everyone is pulled in.
Signals: Early signals include an abrupt, significant price drop by one player that is out of line with
historical pricing or cost logic. For example, a product selling stably at ~£50 suddenly has an offer at
£35 from a competitor – far below typical margin. If other competitors quickly match or beat that price,
you have the makings of a war. You’ll notice accelerating price changes: what was monthly or weekly
adjustments becomes daily or hourly. The direction is uniformly downward and the magnitude of cuts
can increase (e.g. first 10% off, then 20%, etc.). There may also be telltale communications or market
chatter (an announced “price match guarantee” or public statements like “we refuse to be undersold!”)
signaling competitive posturing. An irrational actor often can be identified by their disregard for selfharm
– for instance, continuing to drop prices even as their own stock sells out quickly or their ratings
suffer due to cheapening of service.
Evolution: If unchecked, a price war can drive prices to unsustainably low levels, eroding profit for all.
A famous example: in the 1992 airline price wars, major carriers kept undercutting each other’s fares,
leading to increased sales volume but record losses for the industry . Typically, a war ends when
one of a few things happen: (a) one competitor “wins” by outlasting others – e.g. others capitulate or
exit the market, allowing the survivor to raise prices later (this is rare and often Pyrrhic, as the “winner”
has incurred heavy losses); (b) competitors realize the futility and tacitly agree to stop cutting further
(essentially calling a truce to stabilize prices at a new lower level); or (c) external intervention or market
change occurs (e.g. regulator warning, cost shock making low prices impossible). During the war, the
market sees chaotic behavior: inventory might sell out rapidly at low prices, stockouts can occur, and
some players might secretly hold back (if they can’t profit at the low price, they might stop supplying
until prices recover – leaving the fight to those willing to bleed cash).
Intervention: Avoiding and managing price wars is a critical strategic skill. If an irrational price cut
occurs, do not automatically reciprocate with equal force – first assess if the competitor is in it for
the long haul or if this is a short-lived play (error or promo). As pricing experts advise, “Don’t
immediately lower your prices… reactive slashing can trigger a race to the bottom… destroying margins
without guaranteeing sales” . Instead, attempt to decode the motive behind the competitor’s cut
: Are they offloading stock? Do they have a cost advantage? Are they testing you? Your response
should be proportional and well-justified. Sometimes, a measured response or none at all is best if
you suspect the competitor will pull back (the “promo misunderstood as war” scenario ). If a
response is needed (e.g. key customers are defecting en masse), consider non-price levers first:
emphasize your product’s value, bundle extras, or highlight faster delivery and service – anything to
avoid joining a pure price slashing contest. If you must adjust price, do it surgically: maybe price-match
just on the channel where the war is happening or for specific customer segments (like offer targeted
loyalty discounts) rather than a public across-the-board cut. Internally, set a walk-away point – a price
below which you refuse to go, even if it means losing the sale, because below that point the business is
not worth having. Also, prepare a comeback plan: often after a war, prices rebound (because they were
