4. Cycling Aggressor (Hit-and-Run Undercutter)
Description: A cycling aggressor is a competitor who enters the market periodically with aggressive
pricing, then exits, only to return later and repeat the behavior. This could be a seller who appears when
they have stock (undercutting everyone), sells out quickly, then disappears until they restock. It’s a
boom-bust presence: when they’re active, they disrupt pricing; when they’re gone, the market reverts
to normal.
Signals: Watch for a competitor whose price chart shows intermittent activity: e.g. every few weeks or
months, they list inventory at very low prices, driving the market price down, but they don’t sustain
continuous presence. You might notice that at certain times, the lowest price on a listing is far below
typical, offered by a seller that wasn’t there before. After a short period (days or weeks), that seller is
gone (perhaps sold out or inactive), and prices bounce back up as remaining competitors raise prices
once the outlier is gone. Re-entry behavior is key: the same seller or a new one with similar profile
returns later and does it again. Perhaps they are an opportunistic importer or a liquidation reseller who
cycles through products. Also, inventory signals can hint at this scenario: if you can monitor stock, you’d
see that aggressor’s stock levels drop rapidly during their undercutting phase.
Evolution: During each cycle, the aggressor’s impact is similar to Scenario 1 (Single Aggressor) but
transient. Other competitors may learn the pattern and start anticipating the cycles. For example, savvy
sellers might refrain from price matching a cycling aggressor, knowing “they’ll be gone next week; no
need to slash our price for a short-lived undercutter.” If competitors have learned this, the aggressor
might not actually force the whole market price down, but merely sell out their allotment quickly. After a
few cycles, some competitors might even prepare “war chests” – e.g. hold back some inventory or
budget to deal with the aggressor’s next entrance, or set rules to temporarily lower price only while the
aggressor is active (then bounce back after). It’s also possible the cycling aggressor eventually stops if
they can’t make profit or if they drive themselves out. However, if the aggressor is making strategic
sense (e.g. they are clearing inventory periodically), the pattern might continue indefinitely.
Intervention: The first step is recognition. If you identify a competitor as a cyclical aggressor, you can
avoid overreacting. Instead of panicking each time they appear, plan for it. One strategy is temporal
pricing: during the aggressor’s known active periods, adjust your strategy (perhaps lower your price
modestly to stay competitive for the duration, or offer special deals to loyal customers to retain them),
but be ready to restore prices as soon as the aggressor exits (don’t let a temporary price cut become
permanent). Communicate with internal stakeholders (or your pricing team) that these cycles are
expected; this prevents knee-jerk directives like “match that price now!” from higher-ups who might not
realize the pattern. If data allows, you could even attempt to predict the cycles (for example, every end
of quarter this seller flushes inventory). That can inform stock decisions (maybe delay your own
clearance sale until after theirs, etc.). Another tactic is differentiation: make your offer stand out in
ways beyond price when the aggressor is active. If you can’t beat £X price, can you bundle something
(free accessory, extended warranty) to justify why your slightly higher price is a better deal? This
appeals to customers who might otherwise be lured solely by the low price. Ultimately, direct
intervention (price matching) is often not profitable if the aggressor always exits quickly – you’d just
be giving away margin unnecessarily. So a good system design might automatically flag such
