competitors and switch to a “hold price, monitor closely” mode when they’re detected, and perhaps
accelerate repricing back up once they’re gone. Human review remains important: ensure the aggressor
truly exits; if one day they decide to stick around with deep pockets, your strategy might need to
change to Scenario 1 or 3 handling.
5. Cooperative Equilibrium Broken by New Entrant
Description: In some markets, incumbents settle into a cooperative equilibrium – prices are relatively
high or stable, and while there’s competition, no one is rocking the boat. This often arises in oligopolies
or situations of tacit collusion where each firm enjoys decent margins. A new entrant (or a formerly
small player) can disrupt this peace by pricing aggressively lower to gain entry. Because incumbents
were used to a cozy situation, this new entrant’s move can cause a dramatic shift – essentially ending
the tacit agreement and forcing everyone into a competitive mode.
Signals: Initially, a prolonged period of stable or synchronized pricing indicates a cooperative
equilibrium (e.g. all competitors changing prices only infrequently or together at similar times/rates).
Then, the telltale sign is a new competitor listing or a minor player suddenly cutting price
significantly. You might see news or an announcement, e.g., “New seller joins marketplace” or observe
a price that undercuts the previous market price by a large margin out of nowhere. The new entrant
often positions themselves as a discount alternative. For example, in UK grocery retail, Aldi and Lidl
were new entrants that undercut the “big four” supermarkets’ prices significantly, breaking a long
period of high margins those incumbents enjoyed . The established players’ prices had been
relatively higher due to implicit understanding not to go too low; the entrants shattered that by offering
20-30% cheaper baskets. So look for a newcomer with a very different price structure.
Evolution: Once the new entrant proves they are serious (not a fleeting presence), incumbents are
forced to respond or lose share. This can lead to a protracted price war or permanent margin
compression. Using the same example, the UK supermarkets had to drastically cut prices, accept lower
profit margins (down to 2-3%, “the norm” after discounters’ rise) , and even undertake strategic
changes (store closures, mergers) to cope. Initially, incumbents might try targeted defenses (e.g. pricematch
guarantees on key products, increased promotions) to stall the entrant’s momentum. If the
entrant continues to gain ground, a broader repricing across the market occurs – effectively resetting
the price level lower. Over time, the market might reach a new equilibrium, often at lower price points
across the board, unless the entrant is fended off. In some cases, incumbents may focus on other
dimensions (quality, service, brand) to differentiate, tacitly conceding the low-price segment to the
entrant. Another outcome, if the entrant fails to get traction (perhaps due to brand distrust or capacity
issues), is that they withdraw; then prices may creep back up – but usually not all the way to the old high
equilibrium unless the incumbents are confident no other challengers loom.
Intervention: When a new entrant breaks an equilibrium, early detection is vital. If your system
notices a new competitor on your key ASIN or product, especially one pricing unusually low, flag it for
strategic review. The response should be carefully calibrated. Immediately slashing all prices to match
the entrant can backfire – it legitimizes them and trains customers to expect lower prices from
everyone. Instead, consider a selective approach: identify critical products or segments where the
entrant could siphon your core customers and consider defensive price moves there (possibly
temporary “lock them out” prices or bundling to outvalue them). For less critical segments, you might
let the entrant take the low-end customers while you maintain a premium offering. A principle from
game theory comes to mind: punish defectors but not at cost to yourself beyond what’s necessary.
In repeated games, a strategy like “tit-for-tat” – replicating the opponent’s move – is often effective: e.g.
match the entrant’s price cuts on a few key items (signal that you won’t be undercut across the board)
but not everywhere (signal that you still intend to maintain some price premium unless absolutely
