6. “Race to the Bottom” Dynamics
Description: A “race to the bottom” is an uncontrolled downward spiral of prices, usually involving
multiple competitors continually undercutting each other. Unlike a short-term price war, a race-to-thebottom
dynamic can become a permanent feature of a market if participants rely on simplistic
repricing logic. This often happens in marketplaces with many sellers and automated repricers that are
set to beat the lowest price by a small amount. If everyone uses such a rule without restraint, the price
keeps ticking down penny by penny until margins are annihilated. Essentially, it’s a chronic competitive
deadlock where everyone feels compelled to keep prices as low as possible at all times.
Signals: Persistent price erosion over time is a key sign. You might notice that every day or week, the
lowest price hits a new record low, even though costs haven’t changed. There’s also high-frequency
price changing – perhaps prices update multiple times a day as repricers react to each other’s moves.
If you graph the price, it looks like a staircase going downward with very little upward movement.
Additionally, you may identify automated patterns: e.g. Seller X always prices £0.05 below Seller Y within
minutes of Y’s change, and Seller Z then undercuts X by £0.05, etc. Eventually one of them hits the
minimum allowable price or zero margin. Another clue is convergence of prices: all sellers bunch
tightly around the lowest price, with no one maintaining a premium, suggesting they’re all chasing the
bottom. In Amazon terms, if everyone is fixated on winning the Buy Box purely via price, you’ll see
constant repricing until even winning the sale might mean losing money.
Evolution: In the short run, a race to the bottom benefits consumers (they get the lowest prices), but
it’s unsustainable for the businesses. Margins approach zero, and some competitors may drop out or
go bankrupt. If a few drop out, sometimes the race pauses and prices can rebound slightly as the
remaining sellers have breathing room. However, if the market remains crowded, this dynamic can
persist until an external limit is reached – e.g., sellers hit their minimum advertised price or cost floor, or
a platform sets a price floor. In some cases, sellers eventually wise up that nobody wins in this scenario
and may consciously or unconsciously ease off (leading to tacit collusion or a shift to non-price
competition). Notably, algorithmic repricers have at times caused this outcome; if not configured with
guardrails, they will blindly undercut each other. One famous anecdote at the opposite extreme was two
Amazon repricers that kept raising a price exponentially by trying to outdo each other, resulting in a
absurd $23 million book listing – but the more common danger is the downward spiral.
Intervention: To avoid races to the bottom, the pricing system itself must not be naive. A rule like
“always be $0.01 cheaper than the lowest competitor” is a recipe for disaster if multiple competitors do
the same. As one pricing guide notes, competing solely on price can “undermine perceived value
and compress margins” , leaving everyone worse off. Therefore, a smart strategy is to build in
floors and context: e.g., never price below a certain margin percentage, and don’t keep undercutting if
the price is already very low relative to historical norms or your costs. From a competitive intelligence
