angle, try to determine if your rivals are using automated repricers – their rigid behavior can be
exploited. For instance, if you suspect a competitor will always drop to undercut you, you might
strategically allow them to win some sales at a bad price (they hurt themselves more) while you
hold your price for the more value-conscious buyers. Another tactic is to differentiate: emphasize nonprice
factors like superior shipping (remember, Amazon values overall offer quality, not just price ) so
you can justify a slightly higher price. If you have a good seller rating and FBA (Prime) fulfillment, you
might win sales without matching the rock-bottom price. Educating the team (or clients) is also key:
sometimes management demands “we must be the cheapest!” – but you should present data showing
that a continual undercutting war will wreck profitability and perhaps even cause stockouts or customer
service issues (as ultra-low prices might force cost cuts elsewhere). In scenario terms, stopping a race to
the bottom often means shifting focus from price to value. Internally, track metrics like price index vs.
competitors and gross margin. If you see margin falling faster than volume is rising, it’s a red flag that
chasing the lowest price is not yielding net benefits. The system could incorporate an “alarm” when
margins drop below X% due to price matching, prompting a human review of strategy. Sometimes the
courageous move is to hold price steady while others undercut each other to death – you may lose
some share short-term, but if competitors exit, you inherit a healthier market after. In sum, intervention
here is about prevention: configure repricing rules with strategy (not pure reaction), and if a downward
spiral starts, quickly introduce a new strategy (bundling, value-add, selective price matching) to break
the cycle. As a practical tip: avoid pricing algorithms that lack memory or context – price-only systems
can amplify an aggressor’s impact because they blindly follow the aggressor down . Ensure your
system remembers past price points and knows when to say “no lower.”
7. Margin Restoration after a Competitor Exit
Description: This scenario is almost the aftermath of other scenarios – when a key competitor known
for low pricing exits the market (or a listing), remaining sellers seize the opportunity to raise prices
and rebuild margins. It’s essentially the bounce-back following a period of aggressive competition. For
example, if a dominant price-cutter goes out of business or an Amazon seller with huge inventory gets
banned, the supply-demand balance shifts and those who are left can price more optimally.
Signals: The obvious signal is news or data that a competitor has exited: their offers disappear, or their
stock goes to zero and they do not return. You might see sudden upward price movements by multiple
sellers soon after – a clear indication that a price suppressant is gone. Monitoring competitor inventory
and availability is useful: if one aggressive competitor hits zero stock, and no one else has inventory at
that low price, the “Buy Box” price will start creeping up as others raise to test where demand
balances out. Another sign is a widening gap between the lowest price and next-lowest price if the
lowest (exiting) seller sells out – for a time there may be a vacuum that others slowly fill by raising their
price floor. Essentially, volatility shifts from downward to upward.
Evolution: Margin restoration typically happens in phases. Initially, right after exit, cautious competitors
might only raise prices slightly – wary of scaring off customers used to the low prices. If sales hold, they
raise further, inching margins up. The market may eventually settle at a new price level that is lower
than the pre-war level (because customers have adjusted their willingness to pay somewhat), but higher
than the rock-bottom wartime price. Sometimes, if the exit is due to an external factor (like regulation
or cost increases), all competitors might uniformly increase prices (this can border on tacit collusion but
is a natural response to cost pressures or reduced competition). An example outside e-commerce: after
a brutal airline fare war, if one airline folds or cuts routes, the remaining carriers often hike fares
substantially on those routes, at least for a while, to recoup lost profits. However, one must be careful –
if prices bounce back too high, it can invite new entrants (the cycle restarts) or draw customer
backlash. So, margin restoration tends to be a measured, somewhat stealthy process (e.g., a few
percent increase every week rather than a sudden 50% jump overnight, to “condition” the market).
