Intervention: For a pricing system, this scenario is a reminder that sometimes the correct action is to
raise prices, not just cut. If data shows a key competitor’s exit, the system should consider whether you
now have pricing power. One best practice is to remove or relax competitive price constraints when
the competitor landscape thins out. If your rules were “don’t be more than 5% above the lowest price,”
and that lowest price was from a now-exited discounter, you might adjust your rule to a higher
percentage or switch to a margin-based rule. Test price elasticity: try small increments up and monitor
volume. If volume holds, that’s a green light to continue until you find the new optimum. It’s also a good
time for humans to verify why the competitor exited (to ensure it’s not a temporary stockout that could
reverse). If it’s a permanent exit, you can more confidently rebuild margin. Another consideration is
customer perception – if customers got used to £10 and you’re now charging £15, expect some
resistance. It might be wise to justify higher prices with messaging (e.g. improved features, local stock
for faster delivery, etc.), though in many cases just the absence of the cheap option is enough for
customers to accept higher prices. Strategically, margin restoration windows can be very profitable –
they’re when disciplined competitors earn back what they lost during price wars. But they can also be
tempting moments for remaining competitors to start quietly coordinating: everyone likes the better
margins, so there’s an unspoken incentive to avoid another price war. Your system should be aware that
competitors might engage in tacit collusion at this stage, meaning you may not need to drop price as
quickly as before because others aren’t either. In essence, the intervention here is opportunistic: as soon
as data confirms an aggressor’s exit, pivot from defense to offense – optimize for profit. This could
even be automated: e.g., a rule “if competitor X (aggressor) is not seen for Y days, gradually raise our
price by Z% per day until we hit target margin or see competitor re-entry.”
8. Silent Coordination (Tacit Collusion) Patterns
Description: Not all competitive outcomes are conflict; some are surprisingly peaceful. Tacit collusion
(or silent coordination) occurs when competitors collectively avoid drastic price competition without any
explicit agreement. They might all keep prices high, or they coordinate price increases over time by
carefully watching each other. This is effectively a stable “truce” scenario. It’s often seen in markets with
few players, repeated interactions, and high barriers to entry – classic oligopolies – but can also
temporarily occur on certain Amazon listings (e.g. sellers realize if they all refrain from undercutting,
they can all enjoy higher profits, especially if the Buy Box rotates among them).
Signals: The hallmark is price stability or synchronized moves that cannot be explained by cost alone.
For instance, if over months you see all three main competitors raise prices by 2% at the start of each
month in near unison, that suggests tacit coordination. Or if no one is discounting even during a
normally promotional period, implying a mutual understanding to maintain margin. Another sign is
price leadership behavior where one firm tests a small price increase and others quickly match it
rather than undercut – this indicates they prefer to move together than compete . In Amazon terms,
you might see multiple sellers consistently listing at the same price (perhaps the MSRP or a comfortable
margin price) and none breaking ranks even if sales slow. Another subtle signal: communication via
actions, like one seller raising price slightly (a signal “let’s all go higher”) and waiting – if others follow,
tacit collusion is in effect; if someone doesn’t, the first might revert back (like an unsuccessful trial
balloon).
Evolution: Tacit collusion can be stable for long periods until something changes. That change could be a
new entrant (Scenario 5), external shock (e.g. raw material cost drop or surge in demand tempting
someone to defect), or simply one competitor deciding to quietly grab more share by lowering price
(defecting from the collusive understanding). Often tacit collusion is supported by “tit-for-tat” type
strategies in repeated games: firms implicitly threaten that if one cuts price (defects), others will
retaliate aggressively (grim trigger: permanent price war as punishment). Thus, everyone is deterred
from cheating, and prices remain elevated. However, algorithmic pricing has raised new wrinkles –
10
10
there’s concern that AI-driven pricing could learn tacit collusion (e.g., refrain from undercutting each
other to maximize long-term profit) without any human agreement, making it hard to detect or
prosecute. Real-world example: petrol stations in some markets have exhibited patterns consistent with
tacit collusion through price signalling (like small price changes to communicate intentions) ,
leading to remarkably similar prices street to street.
Intervention: If you find yourself in a tacitly collusive environment (ethics and legality aside), it’s
actually profitable in the short term – but risky if broken. As a system designer, you should not
explicitly build a collusion strategy (that would be unethical/illegal), but you should recognize that if
competitors are consistently not undercutting, a naive aggressive strategy on your part could trigger a
war that hurts everyone (including you). So, paradoxically, sometimes the best competitive move is to
enjoy the quiet and keep prices where they are. Continue monitoring, though – trust is fragile. One
competitor might quietly start to edge prices down to see if they can gain volume without waking the
others. Your system should catch even small defections. A recommended approach is a “tit-for-tat”
response model: remain cooperative (keep price stable) as long as others do, but if a competitor drops
price significantly, respond in kind to signal you won’t be taken advantage of. Essentially, match
reductions to nullify their gain, which discourages future cheating (this mirrors tit-for-tat game theory:
reward cooperation with cooperation, punish defection with defection). At the same time, avoid being
the first to drop price if you believe tacit collusion is in place; it likely will erode your margin and
prompt others to follow or retaliate hard – a lose-lose. It’s worth noting the compliance and legal risk:
while tacit collusion isn’t a formal agreement, regulatory agencies watch for patterns of “conscious
parallelism.” As a guardrail, document that your pricing decisions are based on independent business
rationale (cost, value) even if they end up aligning with competitors’. If you ever embed algorithmic
learning, put in ethical checks so it doesn’t explicitly learn “maximize profit by keeping price high
collectively” in a way that could be seen as collusive. Finally, from a design perspective, tacit collusion
underscores why memory and history matter: if your system doesn’t remember that for the last 6
months no one has undercut anyone, it might erroneously think a high price is uncompetitive and lower
it. An aware system will note the long stable period as context and be cautious about breaking the
pattern without cause.
9. Inventory-Driven Desperation Pricing
Description: This scenario occurs when a competitor’s pricing is driven not by strategy or market share
goals, but by inventory pressure. For example, a seller has a glut of stock (or a seasonal item going out
of season, or a product nearing expiry) and needs to liquidate. They then slash prices aggressively,
sometimes below cost, purely to move units fast. This can look like an irrational aggressor scenario, but
the key driver is inventory management, not competitive dominance per se. It’s “desperation” because
the seller is willing to sacrifice margin (even take a loss) to avoid inventory holding costs or write-offs.
Signals: Often these price cuts come with time-bound or quantity-bound indicators. You might observe
a sudden price drop accompanied by signs like “clearance sale” messaging or the seller’s inventory level
rapidly declining (if visible). The timing might coincide with end-of-quarter or end-of-season. Another
clue: only certain SKUs or categories see the huge cuts (the ones likely overstocked), while the seller’s
other products remain normally priced. If the seller is a retailer, their website or ads might hint at
overstock (e.g. “Year-End Blowout!”). On marketplaces, an inventory-driven sale might cause one seller
to undercut a specific subset of items in a way that seems disproportionate. Also, their behavior might
be inconsistent over time – extremely low prices for a short period, then back to normal once inventory
is at manageable levels. Essentially, short-lived, deep discounts on specific products suggest this
scenario.
