Cross-Industry Analogies and Case Studies
Competitive pricing behavior in marketplaces isn’t unique to Amazon or retail – many industries have
faced similar dynamics. Drawing analogies can provide valuable insights into how companies respond
strategically. Below are brief case studies and analogies from other industries and domains,
illustrating pricing wars, leader-follower behavior, and strategic signalling:
Airline Pricing Wars: The airline industry offers textbook examples of price war triggers and
tacit coordination. In oligopolistic routes, one airline often acts as a price leader; others typically
follow fare increases or match cuts to avoid losing passengers. However, when an airline
misjudges and cuts fares too deeply (or a low-cost carrier enters a route), a tit-for-tat downward
spiral can ensue. A notable instance was the 1992 U.S. airline price war, where major carriers
kept undercutting each other’s discounted fares, leading to massive losses . Airlines also
demonstrate price signalling – they might announce fare hikes via the Airline Tariff Publishing
Company system and wait to see if others match. If competitors don’t match within a day or two,
the initiator often withdraws the increase, thus preventing a price disadvantage (a form of tacit
coordination). Over time, airlines learned to avoid unnecessary fare wars because “each price
war left them weaker with fewer reserves” . They turned to yield management (differentiating
prices by booking timing and customer segment) as a way to compete on price more
intelligently. Lesson: Airlines monitor each other’s moves closely and learned that matching
every cut is sometimes worse than ceding some seats; this underscores the value of
understanding competitor intent (promotion vs. fundamental move) and the high cost of
overreaction.
Ride-Hailing (Uber vs. Lyft): In ride-hailing, we saw a “post-modern price war” fueled by venture
capital. Uber and Lyft both kept fares low (and driver pay high via incentives) for years, losing
money to acquire customers and market share. Uber in particular lost an estimated $1.2 billion
in just the first half of 2016 during its battles . This war was rationalized by a new goal: scale
and market dominance over short-term profit . Uber’s valuation soared despite losses,
based on expectation of future monopoly pricing or new revenue streams. Indeed, once these
companies became a duopoly in many cities, they dialed back discounts and introduced surge
pricing, effectively raising prices on consumers . This mirrors classic predatory pricing where
deep pockets finance a price war to eliminate competition, then prices rise later. Lesson: In
markets with network effects, “winning by losing money” can be a viable (if risky) strategy .
Identifying an aggressor with such motives is crucial – their endgame is different (financing and
scale) so typical profit-based responses won’t deter them. Regulators have taken note; there are
ongoing discussions about whether algorithmic pricing and the ability of apps to adjust fares
simultaneously could lead to tacit collusion or consumer harm . For a pricing system, the
ride-hailing case emphasizes monitoring frequency of price changes and cross-subsidy signs:
if a competitor consistently underprices across all products, they might be playing a long game
that needs a different strategic response (maybe focus on differentiation, since you can’t outrace
them financially).
Retail Fuel Pricing (Petrol Stations): Local fuel markets often have a clear price leader, like a
major brand station, and other stations that follow within hours. Sometimes the pattern forms
an Edgeworth cycle: one station jumps price up (to improve margin), others slowly undercut
over days (to steal customers) until margins thin, then a jump up again. Tacit collusion occurs in
some areas where stations learn not to undercut beyond a point. A real-world anecdote comes
from a petrol price glitch: one Ohio gas station accidentally posted $0.19/gallon due to a
computer error; the station across the street interpreted this as an aggressive move and
dropped to $0.17, starting a mini price war – which ended only when the first station corrected
