mimic your rival’s last action – often leads to mutual cooperation if both follow it (since any
defection is met with immediate punishment, then return to cooperation). In pricing, that means
you keep prices stable unless competitor cuts, in which case you cut to their level (punishment),
but if they raise back or stop cutting, you also raise back. This generally prevents long price wars
and stabilizes at a cooperative outcome, provided both players are patient and value future
profits. Grim trigger is an even harsher strategy: cooperate until the other defects (cuts price),
then punish by keeping price at rock-bottom forever (or a very long time), essentially “I will never
let you enjoy high prices again.” This threat, if credible, can completely deter competitors from
ever initiating a price war. However, grim trigger is risky because if used, it hurts the punisher too
(permanent low profits). Real firms might implement softer versions (e.g. punish for a period of
time proportionate to the deviation). These game theory concepts have been observed implicitly:
for example, some industrial markets have seen episodes where one firm’s aggressive pricing
led others to respond fiercely (even at their own short-term expense) to send a message,
effectively a grim trigger approach to discourage future undercuts. Lesson: The design of pricing
rules can incorporate reciprocity principles – e.g. if competitor consistently behaves, we maintain
higher margins; if they start a war, we respond fully (even if it’s painful) to show resolve. Yet,
unlike theoretical models, real markets have more than two players and imperfect information,
so a direct tit-for-tat might misfire if you misattribute a price change (see the petrol glitch story
again). Still, game theory reminds us that history and reputation matter: a reputation as an
“aggressive competitor” can itself alter rivals’ behavior (they may avoid your markets or think
twice about undercutting you if you’ve shown willingness to fight). So there is value in
sometimes fighting (to build a tough reputation), and value in sometimes cooperating (to enjoy
peaceful profits) – the art is knowing which game you’re in.
Industrial Competition and Predatory Pricing Cases: Throughout history, there have been
stark cases like Standard Oil in the early 1900s or more recently, large firms in the tech sector
being accused of underpricing to kill startups. A famous modern mini-case: Diapers.com vs.
Amazon (2010). Amazon reportedly slashed diaper prices and offered steep promotions on
diapers when trying to win market share from Diapers.com, which was a VC-backed e-commerce
competitor. Diapers.com couldn’t sustain the price matching and eventually was acquired by
Amazon (after which prices were readjusted). This was cited as an example of a predatory
strategy by a capital-rich incumbent. Similarly, in the UK, supermarkets in the 2000s were
accused of pricing fuel below cost in local areas to push out independent petrol stations. Many
such cases show that regulators often have a high bar to act (they want proof of intent to
recoup losses later ), so usually by the time legal action would be taken, the market
dynamics have played out (with the smaller competitor gone or bought out). Lesson: Predatory
pricing is hard to conclusively identify in real-time, but if you suspect you’re the target of one,
you may need to make tough calls – either find a benefactor (partnership, acquisition) to bolster
your capital, or shift your business model. On the flip side, if you’re tempted to use predatory
tactics, remember Baumol’s rule: one proposal is that if you cut prices to fight an entrant,
perhaps you should commit to keep them low for a long time even after they exit – an
idea to remove the incentive for predation. While not law, it’s a strategic thought experiment:
only cut what you’d be okay sustaining. It underscores that constant yo-yo of price (down to kill,
up to exploit) can tarnish customer trust and invite competitor re-entry or regulation.
In all these analogies, common themes emerge: data monitoring (everyone watches each other’s
pricing or outputs), signal interpretation (distinguishing a real move from a blip), response strategy
(when to fight, when to fold), and the importance of contextual factors (capacity, customer perception,
costs, etc.). A multi-industry perspective helps ensure our marketplace pricing system is not myopic –
for example, understanding airline yield management might inspire differentiating by service level in an
e-commerce price war; understanding tit-for-tat might lead to algorithm rules that mirror that logic for
