Behavioral Signals to Monitor and Measure
Building an effective competitive pricing system requires knowing what to watch. We outline key
behavioral signals that can indicate the roles and scenarios described, focusing on observable metrics
(timing, frequency of changes, etc.) rather than any fixed thresholds. These signals will feed into pattern
recognition – essentially, the system’s “eyes and ears” on the market.
Price Leader Signals: Look for a competitor whose price changes precede others’. This could
be measured by correlation or time-lag analysis: e.g., Competitor A raises or lowers price and
within 24 hours several others follow in the same direction. A leader often doesn’t always have
the lowest price, but they set a reference point. If you find that one seller’s price is statistically a
leading indicator for the rest (others react to it, not vice versa), that’s a strong leader signal. Also,
leaders might have regular timing (e.g. they update prices at 6am daily), because they know
others will check and follow. Monitor the magnitude of their moves too – a confident leader
might make larger price adjustments (assuming others will come along), whereas a follower
might only tweak slightly. Another signal: if a competitor rarely undercuts anyone significantly
but still maintains high sales, it could mean customers accept their price as “the market price” –
that’s an informal leadership by brand or trust. Logging these signals helps decide whose moves
to pay closest attention to. When a leader changes price, your system might treat it as a more
important event than if a minor follower does.
Price Follower Signals: A follower typically shows responsive timing – their price changes
occur shortly after a leader’s, rarely initiated independently. You can measure how often a
competitor’s change appears to be a reaction: e.g., Competitor B changes price within X hours of
Competitor A’s change, in the same direction, and perhaps by a similar amount or to the same
price. If this happens consistently (say 80% of B’s moves are following A), B is a clear follower.
Followers also might exhibit price clustering: always staying at a particular offset (like exactly
matching or always £0.10 above/below someone). They might have infrequent updates
otherwise; if the leader is stable, the follower is stable. Track each competitor’s lag time
distribution – some might follow within hours (aggressive follower), others after days or only on
certain triggers (reluctant follower). Also notice behavior when leader is absent: if the leader
goes out of stock and the follower doesn’t know what to do (their price may drift or they
maintain old price until a new reference emerges), that’s another indication they lack initiative.
Such signals can inform rules like “if competitor X is identified as a follower, perhaps we don’t
need to undercut them – if we simply set our price, they will likely match or stay close, meaning
we can maintain margin leadership.”
Aggressor Signals: An aggressor shows disproportionate price cutting frequency and
magnitude. Signals include being the price floor setter (consistently lowest price), making
downward moves more often than up (or never raising price unless forced by cost). They might
undercut by small increments frequently (to always stay lowest) or occasionally by large jumps
(shock and awe strategy). Track the directionality ratio: % of their price changes that are
decreases vs increases. If a competitor has, say, 90% of changes as cuts, they’re likely an
aggressor. Also measure persistence: do they keep undercutting even when they already have
the lowest price? If they drop price and no one has matched yet but they drop again, they’re
essentially driving down the market proactively – a hallmark of aggressive strategy. Another clue
