17
is multi-product aggression: see if the same competitor is aggressive across many products or
just one. If across many, it might be a general strategy (or a sign of a capital-rich player using
penetration pricing). If just one, it could be situational (like inventory clearance – see other
signals). Monitoring their stock levels (if possible) combined with price: an aggressor might also
be the one running out of stock quickly (if their low price boosts volume), then maybe
reappearing (if cyclical). Logging aggressor signals is crucial because your algorithm might
decide to not automatically follow an aggressor – or to identify when one aggressor’s actions are
influencing the whole market (amplifying effect). It’s often wise to set that competitor aside for
human review or at least not let them single-handedly drag your price down.
Temporary Entrant Signals: A temporary entrant or sporadic seller can be identified by
appearance/disappearance patterns. Signals: a competitor ID that wasn’t in the listing before
suddenly shows up at a very low price, then vanishes after a short stint. You’d want to measure
each competitor’s continuity: days active, and frequency of going out-of-stock or inactive. A
high churn competitor (active for a week, gone for a month, back again) likely falls in this
category. Also note their price behavior: they might drop in with a price way outside the
previous distribution (e.g. everyone was around £100, they list at £70). And they often don’t
respond to others – they have their price goal and stick until stockout. Logging these patterns
allows the system to label “Seller123 is a sporadic clearance seller” for instance, prompting a
different strategy (maybe you choose not to match such sellers, or only price-match if they have
significant stock). Re-entry behavior is key to watch: how often do they come back, and does
their pricing follow a cycle (maybe every time they come, it’s the same low price or even lower
each time). Recognizing a temporary entrant can prevent overestimating their long-term impact.
It’s a call for patience in many cases – so your system could issue an alert like “New low-price
entrant detected, likely short-term (last 3 appearances lasted < 1 week). Suggest monitoring vs
immediate price matching.”
Structurally Competitive Market Signals: This refers to markets that are inherently
competitive, with many players and frequent moves, versus those that are stable. Signals here
include price volatility measures (standard deviation of price over time), frequency of changes
across all competitors, and market concentration metrics (like how evenly sales or share are
distributed). A structurally competitive market might have no single leader – instead you see
constant jockeying. The number of distinct sellers rotating in the Buy Box over a month could be
a metric; if it’s high, the market is structurally competitive (everyone’s trying strategies). Also,
monitor if price changes correlate or are independent: in a fragmented competitive scene, you
might see asynchronous price changes (everyone doing their own thing reacting to local
conditions). In a leader-dominated scene, changes are more in unison. A high level of
bidirectional price changes (some up, some down by different players around the same time)
suggests structural competition – different strategies colliding. When the system detects such
signals, it knows the pricing environment is “high chaos” – thus it might rely more on algorithmic
agility (since manual tracking of so many moves is hard), but also ensure guardrails because
high competition can mean high risk of race-to-bottom. It may also influence data collection
priorities: in a fiercely competitive market, you want as real-time data as possible for the repricer
to act, whereas in a stable market hourly data might suffice.
Timing and Frequency Indicators: Independent of roles, just tracking how often each
competitor changes price and at what times can reveal strategies. For example, some might
only change once a day (maybe manual or a rule-based repricer at a set schedule), others every
few minutes (fully dynamic algorithm). If you know Competitor X updates at :00 every hour, you
could time your changes accordingly (either just after to always counter them, or just before to
perhaps influence their move). Frequency is also a health check: if a normally steady competitor
•
•
•
18
suddenly increases frequency of changes, they may have launched a new repricing tool or
adopted a more aggressive stance – something in their strategy changed. That itself could be an
early warning signal of a more competitive phase incoming. Logging frequency over time for
each competitor can show such shifts.
Directionality and Magnitude Patterns: Chart how often each competitor’s changes are cuts vs
hikes, and the average size of changes. For example, a competitor that mostly raises prices
(perhaps reflecting cost increases or confidence) vs one that mostly lowers (competitive
pressure) are in different strategic positions. Magnitude tells you aggression level too – one who
drops 15% in one go vs one who nudges 1% at a time. Magnitude can also signal error vs intent
(e.g. a drastic 50% cut might be a clearance or mistake). So flag extreme changes for analysis
rather than blindly following – maybe a human can verify if it’s a clearance sale or pricing error.
Persistence and Commitment: If a competitor drops price, how long do they keep it low? Do
they bounce back quickly (suggesting it was a test or they got scared by margin loss) or do they
hold firm (indicating commitment to that price level)? A competitor with a “stubborn low”
pricing (persistently low for a long period) is a different beast than one who temporarily
experiments. Measuring how long a new price holds helps classify whether a scenario is
transient or structural. Also, if someone exits after a price war, how quickly do they come back at
higher price? That re-entry timing can indicate their pain threshold.
Re-entry and Rotation: On marketplaces like Amazon, track which sellers rotate in/out of the
Buy Box. If you see patterns like Seller A always appears when price is high (maybe they only
compete when profitable) and disappears when price gets too low, they’re an opportunistic
competitor. Seller B might always be there but sometimes loses Buy Box due to price – suggests
they don’t chase the lowest price (they might be value-focused). Knowing these tendencies
means you can anticipate reactions: Seller B might not react at all to small price drops (they’ll
yield share instead), whereas Seller A might jump in as soon as the market price is above a
threshold. These are complex signals, but modern data collection can capture a lot of it.
Avoiding Rigid Thresholds: It’s worth emphasizing that these signals are meant to be observed and
learned from, not to set in stone fixed rules like “if competitor drops price by 10%, do X.” The goal is to
feed these observations into a diagnosis of the scenario. For instance, a cluster of signals – one seller
with high frequency, always downward, others lagging – clearly indicates a single aggressor with
followers scenario. The system might then label that situation accordingly and recommend strategies
(which a human or a learning model can formalize into rules). By focusing on patterns and trends
(timing, frequency, direction, etc.), we avoid the trap of chasing every single price change, and instead
build a narrative of competitor behavior that leads to smarter decisions.
Strategic Implications for System Design
All the above insights have direct implications on how we should design our advanced pricing and
competition management system. Here, we translate the learnings into practical design principles
and best practices:
1. Observation Before Automation: A key mantra is “watch, label, then act.” Before setting any
automated repricing rules, the system should observe the market and label competitor behaviors and
scenario contexts. For example, identify who the leader is, which scenario is playing out (from the
catalogue above), and tag competitors (aggressor, follower, etc.). This could involve an initial period of
manual or semi-automated analysis where pricing analysts review data and confirm patterns. Only
•
•
•
19
once patterns are understood do we codify responses. This prevents the naive approach of jumping
straight to automation (which might otherwise misfire badly if the scenario is misunderstood).
Essentially, build a phase of “learning mode” for the system, where it collects data, perhaps suggests
classifications, and a human validates them. Over time, this can transition to more automated pattern
recognition, but the principle remains: observe and understand before you intervene with pricing
changes.
2. Human-in-the-Loop Strategy Formation: Even with a lot of data and potential AI, keep humans
involved in strategy, especially early on and for novel situations. Human managers can bring context
(e.g., knowing a competitor’s likely motives, or that a particular competitor has a history of short-term
tactics). They can also consider the broader business strategy (brand positioning, long-term
relationships, etc.) that an algorithm might ignore. Our system should make it easy for analysts to see
the signals and patterns the algorithm detects (visualizations of price history, competitor actions) and
allow them to adjust or approve suggested rules. By doing this, we ensure explainability – the rules
created are understood and agreed upon by humans, not a black box. This is critical if stakeholders ask
“why did we drop our price so much last week?” – we should be able to trace it to “Because competitor X
did Y and our strategy for scenario Z triggered.” An explainable system builds trust and also ensures we
don’t accidentally encode something unethical or risky (like tacitly collusive behavior) without oversight.
3. Avoid Premature Automation & Hard-Coding: It’s tempting to jump into writing a bunch of “if
competitor price < ours by 5%, lower price by £0.10” rules. But as we’ve seen, static rules can backfire –
they don’t adapt to context (is that competitor a clearance seller? Is this a price war scenario? etc.). So
our design should use conditional logic that factors scenario labels. For instance, one could
implement: “If detected scenario is Race to Bottom, then do not follow every price drop – hold until
margin floor, then exit market if needed.” Or “If competitor is identified as temporary entrant, maybe
set price to match second-lowest instead of the lowest price.” These are more nuanced than a one-sizefits-
all rule. In practice, this might mean the system has modules or states: one for normal competition,
one for aggressive war, one for clearance environment, etc., each with tailored policies. The system
should be able to pause automation when it encounters unfamiliar conditions or conflicting signals.
It’s better to revert to manual control for a time than let an algorithm panic. A classic case: a naive
Amazon repricer once famously started repricing against itself when the only competitor left was its
own second account, driving the price way down. A smart system would have a sanity check – e.g. if our
price is falling rapidly without clear competitor triggers, stop and alert a human.
4. Incorporate Memory and Historical Context: Many current repricers are too myopic – they look at
now, maybe yesterday, but not the history. Our system should record historical competitive interactions:
e.g., “Three months ago, a similar price drop happened and it was a short promotion; competitor
restored price in 2 days – outcome: holding our price led to minimal sales loss and avoided margin
erosion.” This memory can inform current decisions (maybe this is the competitor’s known pattern).
Memory is also crucial for detecting tacit collusion or repeated cycles. It’s essentially what a seasoned
human manager has (“I remember last summer, we had a war on this item and no one won”). By
encoding that, we avoid repeating mistakes. Technically, this could be achieved with a knowledge base
or case library in the system that links situations to strategies and outcomes. It can start manual
(analysts input lessons learned) and grow into an AI that recognizes “we’ve seen something like this
before” and suggests the prior successful approach.
5. Focus on Data Collection & Signal Integration: To make decisions beyond price, we need data on
those other factors – delivery times, stock levels, sales velocity, etc. For Amazon, this means monitoring
not just competitor prices, but whether their offer is Prime or standard, their seller rating, their stock
status (if visible via tricks like adding to cart to see max quantity). The system should integrate these
into its decision criteria. For example, if a competitor has a 5-day shipping and we have next-day, our
20
system could assign an “effective price difference” (maybe a 5% value advantage to us) and not lower
our price unless their price minus that difference is still beating us. This prevents racing a slower
competitor to the bottom unnecessarily. The user specifically noted delivery dates – so yes, that should
be a factor. If someone is much slower or has worse reviews, we can sustain a higher price and still win
sales. The data pipeline for the system must capture these attributes in real time. Also, inventory
signals: if we notice a competitor’s stock is diminishing, maybe we predict they’ll be out soon and avoid
a price war, planning to regain sales after. Collecting public signals like competitor inventory (often
possible in limited ways on Amazon) or at least whether they are in stock or not is valuable. In short,
multivariate decision-making (price is one of several variables) will make the system far superior to
“price-only” logic .
6. Explainability and Scenario-Based Justification: Each automated action the system takes should be
explainable in terms of the scenario or signals that prompted it. For example, an internal log might say:
“Price lowered by 5% on Item123 because competitor XYZ (aggressor role) initiated a 10% price cut and
scenario = Price War. Rule applied: defend market share with moderate cut, avoid underselling beyond
cost floor.” If later a manager asks why margins dropped, you can show this log and the scenario
classification. This also helps refine the rules: if an outcome was poor, you can see which scenario logic
led to it and adjust. Explainability is not just for debugging – it builds strategic understanding.
Over time, stakeholders will trust the system because it’s behaving in ways that align with known theory
or agreed strategy (e.g. “We decided to hold prices because it looked like a short-term promotion by a
minor competitor – and indeed that promo ended in 2 days, confirming our strategy.” All that can be
documented by the system).
7. Why Naïve Repricers Fail (and how to do better): Naïve repricers typically fail because they are too
reactive and too narrow. They often chase the lowest price without considering if the lowest-price
competitor is an outlier or a different value offer. They lack understanding of scenarios like aggressive
strategies, so they inadvertently “amplify aggressors” – if one dumb competitor starts a price cut, the
naive repricer just follows, causing a spiral . They also ignore history – e.g. they might repeatedly
drop price against a competitor who always sells out in 1 day, thus giving away margin needlessly every
time. By contrast, our system’s design is to resist reflexive undercutting. Specifically, it will: a)
incorporate a notion of minimal profitable price (never go below a rational floor just because someone
else did), b) consider non-price factors (so it might not match a slower competitor’s price drop at all,
knowing we still win buy box due to Prime shipping), c) use time filters (don’t respond to an anomaly
immediately; maybe wait a few hours to see if it corrects or if it’s a flash sale that ends), and d) not treat
all competitors equally (we might ignore a brand-new seller with 1 unit in stock and bad reviews –
because beating them yields little benefit). In essence, we avoid the pitfalls of naive repricing by
embedding strategic intelligence – the patterns and principles we’ve discussed – into the automation.
This ensures we don’t amplify harmful moves; instead we aim to absorb or dampen them. As one
pricing expert insightfully put it, companies should ask “What else could you do before responding with
price?” – our system will reflect that philosophy, evaluating alternative actions or even doing nothing
as valid strategies in many cases.
8. Safe Escalation and Controlled Experiments: When competition does heat up and we decide to
fight, do it safely and consciously. That means escalating in steps and monitoring impact. For instance,
rather than dropping to match a price 20% below ours in one go (which might be overkill), test a 5%
drop, see if sales improve or competitor reacts further. Use A/B tests if possible on subsets of products
to gauge competitor sensitivity. The system can incorporate these experiment frameworks: e.g., if
unsure about a response, try on one or two products and review outcomes before rolling out widely.
Also, incorporate time limits (time-boxing) on aggressive strategies: e.g., “We will price at break-even
for 7 days to see if competitor exits; if not, revert and rethink.” This prevents endless wars with no
objective. A well-designed system could automatically restore prices after a set duration unless
