they just stabilize at higher prices (to the detriment of consumers). This is an open question: in
practice, will this emerge on marketplaces? If many sellers use similar repricing algorithms, they
could end up in either destructive cycles (if programmed aggressively) or collusive plateaus (if
programmed to maximize profit with detection of retaliation). We should monitor real-world
cases and perhaps even simulate what happens when multiple AI agents interact. Our system
might need a mode to recognize “market-wide algorithms have settled at a high price” and
decide if we want to take advantage by slightly undercutting (to gain share) or join the stability.
But careful – undercutting could break the dam and lead to war. There’s a strategic dilemma
there which isn’t fully solved in theory yet. Future research or regulation might influence how we
allow our algorithms to operate.
Impact of Platform Rules and Intervention: Marketplaces like Amazon periodically change
their algorithms (for Buy Box, search ranking, etc.) and policies. For instance, if Amazon
introduces a rule that shipping speed is weighed even more, or a new “Amazon Competitive
Price” metric that penalizes prices that are too high relative to off-Amazon, these can alter
competition dynamics. Amazon might also step in to prevent price wars on essential goods (they
sometimes deactivate listings with “fair pricing” violations if prices swing wildly or go too low/
high abnormally). We should ask: what future policies could change the game? Perhaps Amazon
could restrict repricing frequency if they see it harming customer experience. Or they might
anonymize some pricing info to prevent constant undercutting. These scenarios are speculative
but not impossible. Our system must be flexible to adapt to rule changes – maybe by having
toggles for new Amazon features or by not being entirely reliant on a single tactic that Amazon
could disallow.
Cross-Market and Omni-Channel Complexity: We talked mostly about competition within one
marketplace, but many businesses sell across multiple channels (Amazon, eBay, their own site,
etc.). Future scenarios might involve price competition across channels – e.g., competitors
might lower prices on one channel that bleed into another (consumers price-match across
channels). There’s also the possibility of multi-market leader-follower: a retailer might lead price
cuts online and others follow offline or vice versa. How to coordinate pricing strategy omnichannel
is an open challenge. If our system is bespoke for one marketplace, that’s fine, but
ideally strategy is unified. For now, we focus on Amazon UK, but future expansion to other
markets or to global scale might bring scenarios like currency fluctuations being weaponized
(one country’s sellers dumping cheap goods into another’s marketplace). Designing for multimarket
awareness is something to consider in future iterations.
New Entrants of Different Nature: In the future, competitors might not just be other resellers.
Manufacturer-direct listings could appear (brands selling at thinner margins), or algorithmic
arbitrage bots that constantly scan and adjust (imagine a bot that reprices based on forex or
stock levels in real time beyond human reaction). Also, subscription models or bundling might
change competition – if some competitor moves to selling the product via subscription (locking
in customers), they might accept a lower per-unit price, changing how one must compete
(maybe you have to offer subscriptions or value-add too). It’s an open question how these
emerging business models intersect with price competition. Keeping an eye on industry trends
(like the rise of D2C brands or changes in consumer preferences) will be important.
Game Theory Evolution with AI: Repeated game scenarios might shift if AI agents can commit
to strategies or signal in credible ways. For example, an AI repricer might be able to implement a
“grim trigger” perfectly: never raising price again if someone undercuts. That could enforce a
collusive high price. Or conversely, an AI might find a loophole in competitor logic to exploit (like
the earlier mention of tricking a competitor’s repricer). We don’t fully know how complex these
