algorithmic interactions can get. It’s a bit of an arms race – algorithms competing with
algorithms. We should be prepared to update our strategy as this evolves, possibly incorporating
elements of adversarial thinking (like how to fool or avoid being fooled by other algorithms).
Regulatory Changes: Competition authorities are debating how to handle algorithmic pricing.
It’s possible that in a few years, certain practices (even tacit ones) might be regulated. For
instance, authorities might demand transparency or impose rules if they detect that algorithms
avoid price cutting too much (to protect consumers). Or they might penalize companies that
engage in what looks like predatory pricing even if the classic legal standard isn’t met, especially
if a few big players dominate online markets. A scenario could be regulators requiring
marketplaces to have built-in “price war circuit breakers” for essential products (just as stock
markets have trading halts). If something like that happens, our system should integrate with
those mechanisms (e.g., if Amazon freezes price changes or imposes a price floor/ceiling in a
crisis, our system must recognize and respect it). So staying informed on policy trends is key.
Data Privacy and PII: Not a focus here, but worth noting: as we gather more data (about
competitors, customers etc.), privacy laws might constrain certain uses. For example, using
individualized pricing based on personal data might run into GDPR issues. We likely won’t do
that for competition, but it’s an open area in pricing (personalized pricing is controversial and
might be curtailed).
Rivalrous AI: Retaliatory Attacks: A more far-fetched but not impossible future scenario – if
pricing becomes very algorithmic, one competitor might try to sabotage another’s algorithm by
feeding it bad data or false signals. For instance, one could momentarily post a very low price to
trick others into matching, then quickly raise price or run out, thus selling at higher price while
others are now low. Already, we saw a bit of this in the Simon-Kucher “oops war” example with
the gas station glitch – though that was accidental. A malicious actor could do similar
intentionally (like placeholder listings to drag prices). We should consider safeguarding against
reacting to data that might be a mirage. This means maybe verifying competitor moves (was that
price sustained for more than a brief moment? Was there actual sales volume at that price?)
before adjusting. And building some resilience to “too good to be true” situations, possibly with
human verification for extreme cases.
Open Questions: - How to quantify the point at which a competitor’s move is “irrational” vs. just lowcost?
(This affects how we respond – maybe future AI can estimate competitor cost structures or
likelihood of sustainability). - Can we develop a reliable early warning system for price wars? (e.g.,
certain metrics that spike right before a war starts – akin to a storm warning). - How to incorporate
customer behavior more directly? E.g., measuring when customers start stockpiling because of low
prices, or when they start switching brands due to a small price difference. The competitive strategy
shouldn’t ignore the demand side. Future systems might integrate elasticity measurements or even realtime
customer sentiment (are they complaining about price differences on social media?).
These questions suggest areas for further research and iteration. The system we design should be seen
as version 1 of a long learning journey. We will continuously test assumptions, collect outcome data (did
strategy X beat strategy Y?), and refine our model of the competitive environment. It’s advisable to keep
a feedback loop where results of pricing actions are fed back into a knowledge base – e.g., “We tried not
matching competitor Q for product Z and lost 5% share but kept 10% higher margin, net profit was
better – good strategy,” or “We tried a price war with competitor Y, both lost money, and no one gained
share – next time avoid.” These lessons help answer open questions over time with our own data.
