In preparing for the future, flexibility is our ally. The system should be easy to update with new rules or
entirely new modules to handle scenarios we haven’t thought of. And the team using it should have a
mindset of continuous improvement – staying updated on competitive theory (avoiding reinventing the
wheel, as the end goal states), and being ready to incorporate new scenarios without rewriting core
philosophy. That core philosophy likely is: use data and strategic insight to drive pricing, rather than
gut feel or blind automation. That should hold true no matter how the environment changes.
Next Steps for Data Collection and Implementation
Finally, to put all this into practice, we outline the recommended next steps for data collection and
system development:
Gather Comprehensive Competitive Data: Begin logging all relevant data points on
competitor behavior. This includes competitor prices (scraped at a high frequency), stock
availability of key competitors (using techniques like checking “only X left in stock” messages or
observing stockouts), shipping options and speeds (who offers Prime/1-day vs standard), seller
ratings and reviews (to gauge trust level), and historical price trends. Also collect internal data:
our own sales volumes at various price points, inventory levels, costs, and conversion rates when
we have Buy Box vs not. This will allow analysis of price elasticity and the true cost of losing vs
winning sales. Ensure data is stored in a way that can be analyzed for patterns – e.g., a timeseries
database for prices, with ability to join across sellers and items. We might also want to
collect external signals: macro trends (Is there a seasonal demand drop that could make
someone cut price? Are there news of a competitor getting funding or having a clearance sale
event?). Initially, focus on the Amazon UK marketplace as specified, but design schemas that
could extend to other regions if needed.
Implement Signal Detection Algorithms: Once data is flowing, create algorithms to detect the
signals discussed: leader identification (correlation of price moves), follower lags, unusual price
drops (beyond certain sigma thresholds), frequency of changes per competitor, etc. Some of
these can be simple scripts, others might use machine learning clustering to group competitors
by behavior. For example, cluster competitors on features like % of time lowest price, avg price
change frequency, avg undercut amount – see if we naturally get clusters corresponding to
“aggressor”, “steady high-price”, “occasional seller” etc. Build dashboards for analysts to visualize
these signals on key products.
Scenario Classification Framework: Develop a rule-based or ML-based engine that, given
recent market data for a product, classifies which scenario is happening (or at least flags which
ones are likely). Initially, this could be rule-based: e.g., “if one competitor’s price dropped >20%
and at least two others matched within 24h, flag potential Price War scenario”. Or “if no price
changes > ±1% in 30 days among top 3 sellers, flag Tacit Stability scenario.” We can refine these
as we test them. Possibly incorporate human input: allow analysts to label scenarios in historical
data to train an ML model later. The classification doesn’t have to be binary – maybe output a
score or likelihood for each scenario type, which can then inform the strategy module.
Strategy Rules and Playbooks: Based on everything compiled, write down concrete playbooks
for each scenario – essentially the advice we’ve discussed but in actionable terms. For example:
Scenario: Single Aggressor – Playbook: Do not chase the aggressor’s price immediately. Instead,
maintain price unless sales drop by >X%. If sales dip too far, consider targeted discount or
