Scenario: Price War – Playbook: Decide on war budget. If strategic to fight, match prices down to
cost floor, for Y period. Simultaneously, ramp up marketing of advantages (so customers see
non-price value). If war exceeds Y period with no resolution, step back and explore other tactics
or yield low-end to competitor.
Scenario: Tacit Collusion – Playbook: Keep prices stable. Do not undercut unless share drops
significantly. If competitor undercuts slightly, consider matching rather than undercutting
further. Keep communication internal only; justify price moves with cost changes or value adds.
And so on for each scenario.
These playbooks can be encoded as decision-tree logic or guidelines that a pricing manager follows.
The eventual goal is to encode them in the system, but first, document them and get stakeholder buy-in
(“Yes, this is how we want to handle a price war, etc.”).
Develop the Pricing Engine with Guardrails: Start implementing the repricing engine that can
take scenario input and apply the corresponding strategy. Build in the guardrails we
enumerated: margin floors, max frequency of price changes, time-based reversion rules, etc. For
instance, have a configuration: minimum margin = 5% (system will never set price that yields less
than that unless explicitly overridden for a special promotion). Or implement a check that if a
price change of more than X% is suggested, require human approval (to catch errors like a
competitor’s glitch). The engine should also be able to output explanations as discussed, so have
it generate a “reason” with each price change recommendation.
Testing and Simulation: Before going live, simulate scenarios using historical data or controlled
experiments. For example, take a past period where we know a price war happened and see how
the system would have reacted with our rules – would it have done better or worse than what
actually happened? Or simulate a competitor aggressively cutting (maybe even coordinate with a
friendly seller to do a small scale test on a less critical product) to see if our detection and
response works as intended. We can also simulate worst-case: all competitors drop to near zero
– does our system just follow to zero (bad) or hold at floor (good)? Adjust accordingly. Use
sandbox environments if possible (some repricing tools let you run in “advice mode” where they
suggest prices but don’t push them live).
Phased Rollout with Monitoring: Roll the system out gradually – maybe start with just a subset
of SKUs (like one category) or in monitoring mode where it suggests changes but a human okays
them. Closely monitor outcomes: Did sales and profit respond as expected? Are we avoiding
unnecessary price cuts? Perhaps run an A/B test: some products managed by the new intelligent
system, some by a simple rule or manual, to measure improvement. Collect feedback from
pricing managers – is the system’s reasoning understandable, do they agree with the scenario
classifications?
Feedback Loop and Continuous Improvement: Establish a regular review (weekly or monthly)
where the team looks at big events (e.g., any price wars or major moves) and assess how the
system handled them. If something went wrong (we joined a race to the bottom inadvertently,
or we lost too much share by being too passive), refine the rules or thresholds. Over time, as we
gather more data, we might identify new subtle scenarios (maybe a hybrid of ones we listed).
Add those to the playbook. Update the system. Also, keep an eye on competitor behavior
changes – e.g. if a competitor that was a follower suddenly starts cutting more (maybe they got
new backing or new management), we might need to reclassify them. The system could alert
“Competitor X behavior change detected” via its signals if, say, their frequency doubled.
