Data Infrastructure for Future Analytics: Plan to collect not just immediate competitive data,
but results of our own pricing actions. This can feed into advanced analytics or even machine
learning in the future. For example, log every time we undercut vs held price, and what
happened to sales and profit. Over enough trials, we could empirically derive the best responses.
This could eventually evolve into a more automated learning system (reinforcement learning,
perhaps) that suggests optimal strategies for certain conditions. But that’s a future step once we
have robust data – the immediate next step is ensure we’re capturing that data.
Stay Educated and Adaptive: Encourage the team to keep up with research (like the references
we used – HBR articles , Simon-Kucher studies , etc.) and industry news. Maybe set
Google Alerts for “price war [industry]” to see how other sectors are experiencing competition.
Sometimes inspiration comes from outside – e.g. game theory research or new pricing tools on
the market might offer new techniques. We shouldn’t consider the system “finished”; it’s an
evolving competitive intelligence platform.
By following these steps, we’ll ensure that we not only implement a system aligned with today’s known
patterns but also create a foundation for learning and adapting to tomorrow’s challenges. The end goal
remains clear: to avoid reactive, price-only thinking and instead build a scenario-aware, intelligent
pricing strategy that secures both profitability and competitiveness. With diligent data collection and
careful strategy design, we can achieve that, arming our system designers (and ultimately the business)
with decision intelligence that keeps us ahead of the curve in any marketplace showdown.
